            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>in MA the tongue is part of the oral region, which in uberon is treated as the oral opening. consider revising oral opening - oral region equivalence.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:UBPROP_0000007>glossal</obo:UBPROP_0000007>
        <obo:UBPROP_0000007>lingual</obo:UBPROP_0000007>
        <obo:UBPROP_0000008>Many species of fish have small folds at the base of their mouths that might informally be called tongues, but they lack a muscular structure like the true tongues found in most tetrapods</obo:UBPROP_0000008>
        <obo:UBPROP_0000011>The tongue has contributions from all pharyngeal arches which changes with time. The tongue initially begins as swelling rostral to foramen cecum, the median tongue bud.. tongue muscles derive from the somites - http://php.med.unsw.edu.au/embryology/index.php?title=Tongue_Development#Pharyngeal_Arch_Contributions</obo:UBPROP_0000011>
        <rdfs:label>tongue</rdfs:label>
        <foaf:depicted_by rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/Tongue.agr.jpg</foaf:depicted_by>
    </owl:Class>
    


